;; _ssl's SSL objects (queue 209): _SSLSocket over a socket or two MemoryBIOs, SSLSession and MemoryBIO.
;;
;; The pipe adapter. Go's tls.Conn wants a net.Conn it may block on; OpenSSL's callers (ssl.py over a
;; non-blocking socket, asyncio's SSLProtocol over MemoryBIOs) want SSL_ERROR_WANT_READ and an operation
;; resumed by the next call. So each SSL object runs its tls.Conn in a goroutine of its own (the engine) over
;; an in-memory transport: the engine's Read takes what the Python side fed it and, when there is nothing,
;; tells the Python side it wants input and waits; its Write appends to the bytes to send. Control alternates
;; strictly (the Python thread waits on the engine's event with the GIL released, the engine waits on the
;; Python side's resume), so the engine touches no Python object and the transport's buffers have one owner at
;; a time. The Python side feeds the engine one TLS record at a time (its header, then its length), never
;; more, so after an unwrap the plain bytes behind close_notify stay in the socket as with OpenSSL. A callback
;; the engine needs (the SNI callback) runs on the Python thread with the GIL. Writes and close_notify, which
;; never wait for input, are made on the Python thread directly once the handshake is done (tls.Conn's write
;; side is independent of its read side).

